# Vendored L-BFGS-B 3.0

This directory contains the reference implementation of the L-BFGS-B
bound-constrained limited-memory quasi-Newton optimizer, vendored into
libfstats so the native exp-model fit (`ComputeExpFitLBFGSB`, see
`../mt_expfit.f90` and `../../mt/mt_fstats.f90`) no longer needs R's
`optim(method="L-BFGS-B")`.

## Provenance

- Package:  L-BFGS-B, version 3.0 (April 25, 2011)
- Authors:  Ciyou Zhu, Richard Byrd, Jorge Nocedal, Jose Luis Morales
- Upstream: https://users.iems.northwestern.edu/~nocedal/Software/Lbfgsb.3.0.tar.gz
            (scipy carries the identical files under scipy/optimize/lbfgsb_src
             in releases up to v1.11.x)
- Reference: R. H. Byrd, P. Lu, J. Nocedal and C. Zhu, "A limited memory
  algorithm for bound constrained optimization", SIAM J. Sci. Comput. 16
  (1995), no. 5, pp. 1190-1208; and J. Nocedal and J. L. Morales, "Remark on
  Algorithm 778" (2011), ACM TOMS.

## Files and what was changed

- `lbfgsb.f`         -- upstream `lbfgsb.f` (setulb + mainlb + all subordinate
                        routines), verbatim APART from the console-output
                        deviation below.
                        upstream md5: 6ac901ddec34ba8e2dd60729f9ae0feb
- `linpack_subset.f` -- verbatim copy of the upstream `linpack.f`, which
                        already contains only `dpofa` and `dtrsl` (the two
                        LINPACK routines L-BFGS-B needs that are NOT in
                        LAPACK). Renamed to make its "subset" role explicit.
                        upstream md5: 0159ad437b5712adc55a42a85b4e7fcf
- `timer_stub.f`     -- REPLACES upstream `timer.f`; returns a constant 0 so
                        the optimizer is deterministic (see the file header).

### Deviation: unguarded console writes commented out

Both drivers here run the optimizer with `iprint = -1`, which suppresses the
iteration report and the `iterate.dat` file. Three `write(6,*)` calls in the
reference sources are **not** under an `iprint` test, however, and so printed to
stdout from inside `libfstats.so` regardless:

| Routine  | Message                                                     |
| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `lnsrlb` | ` ascent direction in projection gd = ...`                  |
| `subsm`  | ` Positive dir derivative in projection ` (+ ` Using the backtracking step `) |

Both are reachable on ill-conditioned problems -- the `subsm` pair is the message
scipy users know from the same upstream code, which scipy also patches out. They
are commented out here, each marked `MT DEVIATION`, for two reasons: stray text
on stdout corrupts machine-readable CLI output, and MT plans a threaded batch
mode in which several solver calls would write to Fortran unit 6 concurrently.

No control flow changed -- `lnsrlb` still sets `info = -4` and returns, and
`subsm` still takes the backtracking step -- so the caller learns of both
conditions through the normal reverse-communication path. Every other
`write (6,...)` in the file is already `iprint`-guarded and is left untouched.

## Deliberately NOT vendored

- `blas.f` (the bundled `dcopy`/`daxpy`/`ddot`/`dscal`/`dnrm2`). libfstats
  already links the system BLAS (libblas.so.3, transitively via liblinalg);
  bundling a second copy would create duplicate symbols. L-BFGS-B here uses
  only `dcopy`, `daxpy`, `ddot`, `dscal` from BLAS-1, all resolved from the
  linked BLAS. (`dnrm2` is not referenced by lbfgsb.f.)
- The drivers, Makefile, PDFs, and sample outputs from the tarball.
- **netlib PORT `dn2gb` (bounded NL2SOL).** The native bounded exp-model fit
  (`ComputeExpFitPort`) replaces R's `nls(algorithm="port")`. The intended
  vendor source was netlib's `dn2gb` (double-precision NL2SOL with simple
  bounds) from <https://netlib.org/port/>. It was **declined on licensing
  grounds**: unlike the unbounded NL2SOL (TOMS Algorithm 573, whose
  ACM-distributed form carries a permissive license), the *bounded* `dn2gb`
  exists only inside the PORT library, which per <https://netlib.org/port/readme>
  is copyright "1984, AT&T Bell Telephone Laboratories, Inc." and is available
  "without charge for **non-commercial use** [only] under a non-exclusive
  limited-use software agreement" -- commercial use requires a separate Lucent
  license. `dn2gb.f` itself carries no BSD/ACM grant (it only cites the NL2SOL
  paper for the algorithm). Because this repository ships compiled binaries
  (`libfstats.so`), a non-commercial-use-only dependency cannot be embedded.
  `ComputeExpFitPort` is therefore implemented as a **projected Gauss-Newton
  substitute** in `../mt_expfit.f90` (`expfit_gauss_newton_bounded`): the same
  analytic-Jacobian Gauss-Newton as the unbounded fit, with each trial step
  clamped into the box. See the task-5 report for the full gate evidence.

## License

L-BFGS-B 3.0 is distributed under the 3-clause ("New"/"Modified") BSD License.
The full text lives in `License.txt` in this directory -- both `lbfgsb.f` and
`linpack_subset.f` say "Please read attached file License.txt" in their headers,
so that file is present here for the reference to resolve. It also records the
authors, because upstream ships the BSD template with its `<year>` /
`<copyright holder>` / `<organization>` placeholders unfilled.

**Binary redistribution:** clause 2 of the license requires the notice to be
reproduced in the materials provided with a binary distribution, and this
repository ships compiled libraries. `../../THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` is the file
meant to travel with them; see it before packaging a `libfstats.so`/`.dll`. (This
is the same reasoning that ruled out vendoring netlib PORT above -- it applies to
what *is* vendored too.)

